  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14269988/assets/b65fc4830e0b060d62226b41d5ccb7bdc63d0ad594f7a42a2628597409bba3af.png)\n\n      \n\nRecall the following facts regarding the congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n):$\n\n- The congruence has no solutions modulo $n$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,b.\n$$\n\n- The congruence has $\\text{gcd}(a,n)$ solutions modulo $n$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)\\,|\\,b.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our congruences.\n\n- Congruence I has no solutions. We have $a=4$ and $n=20$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,20)=4.\n$$\n\nSince $b=9$ is not divisible by $4,$ this congruence has no solutions.\n\n- Congruence II has four solutions modulo $20.$ We have $a=12$ and $n=20$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(12,20)=4.\n$$\n\nSince $b=16$ is divisible by $4,$ this congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\n- Congruence III has four solutions modulo $20.$ We have $a=8$ and $n=20$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,20)=4.\n$$\n\nSince $b=12$ is divisible by $4,$ this congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
